      "knowledge_point_id": "6b65b190-b587-5827-a160-b8df85b6227c",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Scalar Curl of a Vector Field",
      "problem": "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=(x+{y}^{2})\\,i+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,j.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $F$ at the point $(0,-1).$",
      "worked_solution": "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1))-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1}-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(0,-1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(0,-1)} & =\\frac{2(0)(-1)}{{0}^{2}+1}-2(-1) \\\\  & =0+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
